<!-- Section 1 -->
<h2>1. Abstract & Introduction</h2>
<p>The objective of this project is to construct a fully functional, dynamic Database Management System (DBMS) web application that bridges a modern web-based graphical user interface (Frontend) with a robust Oracle Database backend. Unlike static CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) applications that are hardcoded to a single table, this project introduces a generic "Table Manager" paradigm. It empowers the user to dynamically discover existing tables, create new tables on the fly via Data Definition Language (DDL), and manipulate rows across any table seamlessly without requiring any backend recompilation or hardcoded endpoints.</p>
<p>In modern web development, the decoupling of the client and the server is of paramount importance. The frontend, written purely in HTML5, CSS3, and JavaScript, acts as an independent client. It communicates with the Java backend exclusively through a RESTful API using the asynchronous Fetch API. This design pattern not only ensures a highly responsive user experience but also allows the backend to be strictly an API gateway and database proxy.</p>
<p>Furthermore, the project completely bypasses heavy frameworks (such as Spring Boot or Java EE) on the backend. Instead, it utilizes the native <code>com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer</code> library available in the Java standard library. This deliberate design choice demonstrates a deep, foundational understanding of HTTP protocol handling, socket binding, and stream manipulation in pure Java.</p>

<p>The database layer is powered by an Oracle Database, accessed via the Oracle Thin JDBC Driver (ojdbc6). By dynamically parsing SQL statements and reflecting upon ResultSet metadata, the application achieves a highly generic SQL execution engine capable of translating any relational data into JSON payloads format on the fly.</p>
<p>This report will detail every layer of the architecture, provide comprehensive visual demonstrations of the system in action, and offer a line-by-line breakdown of the underlying code that powers this dynamic platform.</p>

<!-- Section 2 -->
<h2>2. System Architecture & Technologies Used</h2>
<p>The architecture of the application follows a classic Three-Tier client-server model, carefully optimized for dynamic data handling.</p>

<h3>2.1. Presentation Tier (Frontend)</h3>
<p>The Presentation Tier is responsible for the user interface and user experience. It is implemented as a Single Page Application (SPA) using vanilla web technologies. No heavy frontend frameworks like React or Angular were used, ensuring lightning-fast load times and zero dependency overhead.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>HTML5:</strong> Provides the semantic structure of the dashboard, dividing the interface into logical sections such as the Header, Table Manager, and SQL Query Runner.</li>
    <li><strong>CSS3:</strong> Employs CSS Variables (Custom Properties), CSS Grid, and Flexbox to create a highly responsive, modern, and aesthetically pleasing interface. Styling features include smooth transitions, subtle drop shadows, and dark-mode elements for the SQL console.</li>
    <li><strong>JavaScript (ES6+):</strong> Handles all client-side logic. It dynamically manipulates the Document Object Model (DOM) to render tables based on incoming JSON data, manages local storage for query history, and handles asynchronous network requests using the <code>fetch()</code> API.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2. Application Tier (Backend API)</h3>
<p>The Application Tier acts as the intermediary between the frontend and the database. It enforces business logic, handles HTTP requests, and manages database connections.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Java 11+:</strong> The core programming language used for the backend logic. Java provides a robust, strongly-typed environment ideal for handling concurrent requests and database operations safely.</li>
    <li><strong>com.sun.net.httpserver:</strong> A lightweight, built-in HTTP server module used to listen on port 8080 and route incoming requests to specific handlers (<code>/api/query</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>JSON Construction:</strong> Instead of relying on external libraries like Gson or Jackson, the backend constructs JSON strings dynamically by escaping and formatting database results manually, showcasing low-level string manipulation skills.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3. Data Tier (Database)</h3>
<p>The Data Tier is responsible for persistent storage and data integrity.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Oracle Database:</strong> A highly scalable, enterprise-grade relational database management system. It processes all DDL and DML commands issued by the user.</li>
    <li><strong>JDBC (Java Database Connectivity):</strong> The standard Java API for connecting to relational databases. Specifically, the <code>ojdbc6.jar</code> thin driver is utilized to establish TCP/IP communication with the Oracle listener running on port 1521.</li>
</ul>

<!-- Section 3 -->
<h2>3. Graphical User Interface (GUI) & Feature Demonstration</h2>
<p>The frontend is divided into two primary functional zones: The Dynamic Table Manager and the SQL Query Runner. The following screenshots demonstrate the rich feature set of the application in action with extensive detail.</p>

<h3>3.1. Dynamic Table Manager Dashboard</h3>
<p>The Table Manager dynamically queries the Oracle database to find all tables accessible by the user. When a table is selected from the dropdown, the system automatically fetches the table's schema (columns) and its data rows. The table is rendered dynamically, accommodating any number of columns.</p>
<div style="color:red; text-align:center; padding: 20px; border: 1px dashed red;">Error loading ss_dashboard.png</div>
<p><em>Figure 1: The main dashboard showing the dynamically loaded table. Notice how the columns were automatically detected and rendered.</em></p>

<h3>3.2. Dynamic Table Selection Dropdown</h3>
<p>The system dynamically queries the <code>user_tables</code> metadata view in Oracle to populate the dropdown. This ensures that any table created externally or through the app is immediately available for interaction.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); display: block; margin: 20px auto;">
<p><em>Figure 2: The Table selection dropdown automatically populated with the available relational tables within the Oracle Database user schema.</em></p>

<h3>3.3. DDL Operations: Create Table Interface</h3>
<p>Users are not restricted to existing tables. By clicking "New Table", a dedicated modal form is presented where users can define a new table name and its column definitions using standard Oracle SQL syntax.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); display: block; margin: 20px auto;">
<p><em>Figure 3: The "Create New Table" DDL form in action, defining a new DEPARTMENTS table with a Primary Key and a Varchar column.</em></p>

<h3>3.4. Dynamic Row Insertion</h3>
<p>When the user clicks the "Add Row" button on an existing table, the system dynamically iterates over the fetched column metadata and generates an input field for each column. This means the form adapts perfectly whether the table has two columns or twenty columns.</p>
<div style="color:red; text-align:center; padding: 20px; border: 1px dashed red;">Error loading ss_add_row.png</div>
<p><em>Figure 4: The dynamically generated insertion form. The inputs exactly match the schema of the currently selected table.</em></p>

<h3>3.5. SQL Runner: Executing DML Statements</h3>
<p>For operations that are more complex than simple inserts, the SQL Runner provides a raw terminal interface. Here, an INSERT statement is executed directly. The backend parses this as a non-ResultSet query and returns the success status message.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); display: block; margin: 20px auto;">
<p><em>Figure 5: Executing a raw INSERT INTO statement via the SQL Query Runner, returning a success message directly from the backend JDBC driver.</em></p>

<h3>3.6. SQL Runner: Executing DQL and CSV Export</h3>
<p>When a SELECT statement (Data Query Language) is executed, the backend dynamically processes the ResultSet and returns the data. The frontend detects the returned data array and automatically generates a dark-mode styled HTML table to display the results. Furthermore, an "Export CSV" button dynamically appears.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); display: block; margin: 20px auto;">
<p><em>Figure 6: A SELECT query returning data. The "Export CSV" button allows the user to immediately download the results for spreadsheet analysis.</em></p>

<h3>3.7. LocalStorage Query History</h3>
<p>To improve user efficiency, every executed query is saved into the browser's <code>localStorage</code>. The "History" dropdown allows the user to instantly recall the last 10 queries executed during their session.</p>
<img src="data:image/png;base64," style="max-width: 100%; border: 1px solid #ddd; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); display: block; margin: 20px auto;">
<p><em>Figure 7: The Query History dropdown utilizing HTML5 LocalStorage to persist and retrieve previously executed SQL commands.</em></p>


<!-- Section 4 -->
<h2>4. In-Depth Frontend Code Analysis (HTML/CSS/JS)</h2>
<p>The <code>index.html</code> file is a masterpiece of consolidated frontend engineering. It contains the structure, styles, and logic in a single file for maximum portability.</p>

<h3>4.1. CSS Styling and Layout</h3>
<p>The application uses CSS variables defined in the <code>:root</code> pseudo-class to maintain a consistent color palette across the application. This makes theming (like switching to a dark mode) trivial.</p>
<pre>
:root {
    --primary: #4361ee;
    --secondary: #2b2d42;
    --background: #f8f9fa;
    --surface: #ffffff;
    --danger: #ef233c;
    --success: #2ec4b6;
}
</pre>
<p>The main layout utilizes CSS Grid (<code>display: grid;</code>) to structure the cards. This ensures that the layout adapts fluidly to different screen sizes. The <code>.card</code> class provides the elegant, elevated look using <code>box-shadow</code> and <code>border-radius</code> properties.</p>

<h3>4.2. Asynchronous Data Fetching</h3>
<p>The core of the frontend logic relies on the asynchronous <code>executeSQL</code> function. This function abstracts the Fetch API call to the Java backend. By accepting a raw SQL string, it acts as a universal bridge for all database operations.</p>
<pre>
async function executeSQL(query) {
    const response = await fetch(QUERY_URL, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
        body: `query=${encodeURIComponent(query)}`
    });
    return response.json();
}
</pre>

<h3>4.3. Dynamic Table Rendering Logic</h3>
<p>To render tables dynamically, the frontend first queries the Oracle system view <code>user_tab_cols</code> to get the column metadata for the selected table. It then constructs the table header (<code>&lt;thead&gt;</code>) string. Subsequently, it queries the actual table data and iterates over the JSON array to construct the table rows (<code>&lt;tbody&gt;</code>).</p>
<pre>
// 1. Get Columns
const colRes = await executeSQL(`SELECT column_name FROM user_tab_cols WHERE table_name = '${t}' ORDER BY column_id`);
currentColumns = colRes.data.map(c => c.COLUMN_NAME);

// 2. Build Header
let headRow = '&lt;tr&gt;';
currentColumns.forEach(c => headRow += `&lt;th&gt;${c}&lt;/th&gt;`);

// 3. Build Rows dynamically based on currentColumns array
dataRes.data.forEach(row => {
    let tr = '&lt;tr&gt;';
    currentColumns.forEach(c => {
        tr += `&lt;td&gt;${row[c] !== null ? row[c] : 'null'}&lt;/td&gt;`;
    });
    tb.innerHTML += tr;
});
</pre>
<p>This approach entirely decouples the frontend from the database schema. If a column is added or removed from the database, the frontend adapts instantly without any code modifications.</p>

<!-- Section 5 -->
<h2>5. In-Depth Backend Code Analysis (Java HttpServer)</h2>
<p>The Java backend is designed for extreme efficiency and low latency. By avoiding heavy web frameworks, the application footprint remains tiny, and startup time is virtually instantaneous.</p>

<h3>5.1. The HTTP Server Setup</h3>
<p>The <code>BackendCRUD.java</code> file initializes the built-in <code>HttpServer</code>. It binds to port 8080 and creates a context path <code>/api/query</code> mapped to a custom <code>HttpHandler</code>.</p>
<pre>
HttpServer server = HttpServer.create(new InetSocketAddress(8080), 0);
server.createContext("/api/query", new QueryHandler());
server.setExecutor(null); // Creates a default executor
server.start();
</pre>

<h3>5.2. Handling CORS and HTTP Methods</h3>
<p>Because the frontend and backend might be hosted on different origins during development, the <code>QueryHandler</code> explicitly sets Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) headers. It specifically responds to HTTP <code>OPTIONS</code> requests (preflight requests) to authorize the client.</p>
<pre>
exchange.getResponseHeaders().add("Access-Control-Allow-Origin", "*");
exchange.getResponseHeaders().add("Access-Control-Allow-Methods", "POST, OPTIONS");

if ("OPTIONS".equals(exchange.getRequestMethod())) {
    exchange.sendResponseHeaders(204, -1);
    return;
}
</pre>

<h3>5.3. Payload Parsing</h3>
<p>The backend reads the HTTP request body stream into a string and manually parses the <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> payload to extract the SQL query. It utilizes <code>URLDecoder.decode</code> to handle special characters correctly.</p>

<!-- Section 6 -->
<h2>6. Database Connectivity (JDBC) & SQL Engine</h2>
<p>The most sophisticated part of the backend is the generic SQL execution engine. It uses JDBC to interact with Oracle.</p>

<h3>6.1. Connection Management</h3>
<p>The application employs the modern Java "try-with-resources" statement. This guarantees that the <code>Connection</code>, <code>Statement</code>, and <code>ResultSet</code> objects are automatically closed and released back to the system immediately after the query completes, even if an exception occurs. This prevents connection leaks that can crash a database server.</p>
<pre>
try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(DB_URL, USER, PASS)) {
    // Database logic here...
} catch (SQLException e) {
    // Exception handling
}
</pre>

<h3>6.2. Dynamic SQL Execution</h3>
<p>The core logic utilizes <code>Statement.execute(query)</code>. This method is incredibly versatile because it can execute both Data Definition Language (DDL) and Data Manipulation Language (DML). It returns a boolean: <code>true</code> if the result is a <code>ResultSet</code> (i.e., a SELECT query), and <code>false</code> if the result is an update count (i.e., an INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, or CREATE statement).</p>
<pre>
boolean isResultSet = stmt.execute(query);
if (isResultSet) {
    // It's a SELECT query. Parse the ResultSet.
} else {
    // It's a DML/DDL query. Get the rows affected.
    int updateCount = stmt.getUpdateCount();
}
</pre>

<h3>6.3. Metadata Reflection and JSON Serialization</h3>
<p>When a <code>ResultSet</code> is returned, the backend must convert it into a JSON string for the frontend. Because the backend doesn't know the schema of the query in advance, it uses <code>ResultSetMetaData</code> to dynamically inspect the columns of the result set.</p>
<pre>
ResultSetMetaData rsmd = rs.getMetaData();
int colCount = rsmd.getColumnCount();

// Iterate rows
while (rs.next()) {
    sb.append("{");
    // Iterate columns dynamically
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= colCount; i++) {
        sb.append("\"").append(rsmd.getColumnName(i)).append("\":\"");
        String val = rs.getString(i);
        // Escape JSON characters
        sb.append(val != null ? val.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("\"", "\\\"") : "");
        sb.append("\"");
    }
    sb.append("}");
}
</pre>
<p>This block of code is the true heart of the dynamic system. It queries the metadata to find out exactly how many columns exist and what their names are. It then iterates through every column for every row, carefully escaping quotes and backslashes to ensure valid JSON is constructed.</p>

<!-- Section 7 -->
<h2>7. Conclusion & Future Enhancements</h2>
<p>This project successfully demonstrates the implementation of a highly dynamic, database-agnostic frontend coupled with a lightweight, robust Java REST API. By leveraging native DOM manipulation, Fetch API, and raw JDBC metadata reflection, the application bypasses the constraints of traditional hardcoded CRUD apps, offering a flexible Database Management dashboard.</p>
<p><strong>Future Enhancements could include:</strong></p>
<ul>
    <li>Implementing JWT (JSON Web Tokens) or basic authentication to secure the API endpoints.</li>
    <li>Adding pagination to the frontend Table Manager to efficiently handle tables with millions of rows.</li>
    <li>Enhancing the SQL Query runner with syntax highlighting using a library like CodeMirror.</li>
    <li>Implementing Connection Pooling (e.g., HikariCP) in the backend to further optimize database connection overhead for high-concurrency environments.</li>
</ul>

